{"edits":[{"content":"{\"analogy\":null,\"claim\":\"As n grows, the probability of reaching unanimous consensus tends to 0. The finite obstruction structures are locally stable mixed configurations in which every vertex has exactly two opposite-color neighbors, so the set of crossing edges is a disjoint union of cycles and each monochromatic induced subgraph has minimum degree 0 or 1. The expected number of such stable mixed configurations grows like exp(c n) for some c>0, so the probability that the dynamics avoids all traps and reaches consensus is at most exp(-c n).\",\"counterconditions\":[{\"case\":\"If the basin of attraction of the two consensus states occupies a 1-o(1) fraction of configurations despite exponentially many stable mixed states, then consensus probability would tend to 1.\",\"checker_spec\":null,\"eval\":\"observation\"},{\"case\":\"If stable mixed configurations are not reachable from typical initial colorings because their basins are exponentially small, the conclusion fails.\",\"checker_spec\":null,\"eval\":\"observation\"}],\"definitions\":[],\"derivation\":[],\"formalization_refs\":[],\"mechanism\":\"Each vertex updates to the minority color among its three neighbors, strictly increasing the number of bichromatic edges until no vertex has fewer than two opposite-color neighbors. Stable mixed configurations are exactly 2-2 cuts whose crossing edges form a disjoint union of cycles; on a random 3-regular graph there are exponentially many such cuts because one can independently choose cycle components of each color and match them across a sparse set of edges. Starting from a uniform random coloring, the process is likely to hit one of these exponentially many absorbing traps before eliminating all crossing edges, so consensus is exponentially unlikely.\",\"premises\":[],\"schema\":\"deepreason-reasoning-envelope-v1\",\"scope\":{\"covers\":[\"random 3-regular graphs\"],\"excludes\":[\"bipartite graphs\"]},\"simulation_refs\":[],\"uncertainties